  "prompt_text": "The figure compares eight structural settings where a hypothetical geothermal system is most likely to occur relative to faults, shown by red ellipses with a blue star marking an optimal well site. Across all examples, the main message is that geothermal upflow tends to localize where fault geometry becomes more complex and fractured, rather than along simple, isolated fault segments.\n\nThe examples include fault terminations, step-overs or relay ramps, fault intersections, accommodation zones, displacement transfer zones, transtensional pull-aparts, intersections between faults and a caldera margin, and dike tips. In each case, the highlighted target sits in zones that appear to concentrate strain, extension, or rock damage, such as overlapping faults, connecting faults, fault tips, and caldera-margin fractures. Several panels explicitly indicate abundant fracturing, breccia, or enhanced extension, reinforcing that permeability is expected to be greatest in these areas.\n\nA gray line marks the cross section in each map view, and the narrow lower strips show simplified subsurface geometry beneath that line. These cross sections suggest steep structures focusing flow upward into the red target zones. Overall, the figure is a conceptual guide for siting geothermal wells in structurally favorable, highly fractured areas.",
